{
  "requirement": "For every `{impl}`, `{cite}`, and `{test}` reference in a project source file, the evidence file for the referenced requirement or example MUST contain an input whose resolved source ranges cover the reference's file and line. Habeas MUST report every uncovered reference with its role, full name, source file, and line number.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: the requirement is met.**\n\n**Implementation**\n- `collect_references` in `src/evidence.rs` scans every project file except the root-level `.git`, `target`, `book` and `evidence` directories. It skips binary files. For each `{impl}`, `{cite}` or `{test}` role it records the full name, role, file and 1-based line.\n- `validate_references` groups the references by their target requirement or example. For each definition it loads that definition's `evidence/<name>.json` through `evidence_input_ranges`.\n- A reference is covered only if some input has the same file and `line_start <= line <= line_end`. Otherwise it pushes the error `\"<evidence file>: no input covers {role}`full.name` at <file>:<line>\"`. That message contains the role, full name, source file and line, as the requirement demands.\n- `evidence_input_ranges` resolves every input selector, whether a plain file range, a Rust item or a requirements glob. It uses the resolved `SourceRange` values, so coverage is judged on resolved ranges and not on the raw JSON fields. Both sides are canonicalized: `inspect_repository` canonicalizes the root, and the input ranges are canonicalized too. Paths therefore compare consistently.\n- Unresolvable selectors and missing evidence files produce no ranges. The references are then reported as uncovered, which is the safe outcome.\n\n**Tests**\n- `source_references_are_covered_by_evidence_inputs` is the positive case. `{impl}` and `{cite}` references in `src/main.rs` are covered by a file range. A `{test}` reference in `md/notes.md` is covered by a range in a different file. `{cite}` examples inside code spans and fences are correctly ignored. No errors result.\n- `reports_unknown_references_and_uncovered_lines` is the negative case. The evidence covers only line 1 of `src/main.rs`. The test asserts that exactly two errors occur. One is the uncovered `{test}` reference on line 2, asserted as `no input covers {test}`foo.bar.ready` at src/main.rs:2`. The other is the unknown-target error. The covered line 1 produces no error, so the range boundary is exercised.\n- `cited_example_with_a_test_and_matching_evidence_validates` covers Rust-item and requirements-glob inputs as coverage sources. It blanks the inputs and asserts that `no input covers` and `src/main.rs:1` appear in the errors. It also checks that changed input text is detected.\n\n**Gaps:** None are apparent, and I found no FIXME markers. The tests cover covered, uncovered, cross-file and resolved-selector cases, and they check the reported role, name, file and line.",
